@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+Finding: synthetic scanner / SEED-SHELL-TRUE / runner.py:6 (original location) / P1, SEC-001; evidence: finding.json.
+
+Confirmed: yes, by source tracing. Caller-controlled name was interpolated into a command passed to subprocess.run with shell=True, allowing shell metacharacters to introduce commands. No payload was executed.
+
+Remediation: greet now passes an argument list with shell=False. The name remains a single literal argument, removing shell interpretation while preserving the function signature and stripped stdout return value.
+
+Verification path: behavioral regression (execution pending in hosted CI).
+
+Regression test: test_runner.py::GreetTests::test_shell_metacharacters_are_passed_as_a_literal_argument uses an inert canary and mocks runner.subprocess.run. It checks literal argument boundaries, disabled shell execution, and the returned greeting. The original implementation would violate the asserted subprocess call. Old-code failure and new-code success are pending, not observed. The existing test_plain_name test is preserved.
+
+Residual risk: the external echo executable is still resolved through the process environment; this change addresses caller-controlled shell injection, not a compromised execution environment. The syntax-only scanner cannot prove behavioral safety. Hosted verification is pending; the finding is not claimed verified or closed.
+
+Verified: source files were read and edited only. No tests, scanners, application code, dependency installations, or network commands were run, per the user's execution constraint.
+
+Pending hosted verification:
